Part 1: Theory & Legal Background (Tasks x & y)

x) Why use a feed reader (RSS/Atom) rather than just visit each website?

Efficiency & Aggregation: A feed reader gathers all the security blogs' feeds into one "River of News" so that you don't have to visit 10 to 20 blogs every day to see if anything new has been posted.

Unlike social media platforms (X/Twitter, LinkedIn) that use engagement algorithms to hide and reorder posts, RSS takes you all of the posts from your selected sources, and shows them to you exactly as they're posted and published.

Privacy & Distraction-Free Reading: A feed reader retrieves the clean XML text without tracking cookies, advertising or clickbait side bars. Also, it maintains the Read/Unread status.

y) Supreme Court of Finland: Case Osuuspankkikeskus Port Scan (KKO:2003:36)

The Incident: An individual ran a port scan of Osuuspankkikeskus (OP Bank Group) firewall/network to determine if any open ports or vulnerabilities exist, but did not try to gain access to or steal its data.

The Ruling: The Supreme Court has ruled that scanning alone is not an offence (from the point of view of the Finnish criminal law it doesn't constitute attempted computer break-in / tietomurron yritys). Scanning only identifies visible points of entry in the network and doesn't yet penetrate security measures or gain access to protected information.

Although it was not proven in this particular precedent from 2003 that port scanning was an attempted break-in, it is still possible for scanning networks that you do not belong to to trigger an intrusion detection alarm, to break an ISP Terms of Service, or be the subject of a legal investigation, so never scan anything other than localhost (or your own approved lab machines).

