Task e) Follow Security Feeds & Reflection

 What I Learned from the Feeds
There were a few themes in the cybersecurity space that surfaced on the collected feeds from Thunderbird on Krebs on Security, Schneier on Security, Microsoft Security, and BleepingComputer:
- Ransomware & Cybercrime Operations: Krebs on Security focussed on ransomware group leadership investigations, ransom negotiators, and botnet takedowns[cite: 13] and illustrated the cybercrime business ecosystem.
Patch Management & Enterprise Vulnerabilities were covered by both Krebs and Microsoft Security, as both had reported on massive monthly patch cycles, including Microsoft patching more than a thousand vulnerabilities and PHP having widespread flaws[cite: 13].
- Cryptography, AI Security, & Privacy: Bruce Schneier's feed featured articles on technical subjects such as the formal verification of the Signal protocol and also on the general topic of AI risks, usable security and digital surveillance[cite: 18].

 Benefits vs. Downsides of Using a Feed Reader

Leverage RSS/Atom feeds or other sources (social media, news sites, newsletters) |

Upstairs: You see posts from trusted sources as they appear in real-time without algorithmic manipulation. | Downside: Algorithmically manipulated posts are shown, outrage/clickbait posts are shown, and irrelevant posts are injected. |
People benefit: can see headlines quickly without leaving the site; clean text free from tracking cookies, pop-ups and ads.People suffer: have to visit individual sites to see headlines; are overrun with tracking cookies, pop-ups and ads. |
DISADVANTAGE: If you miss a couple of days, you'll have a lot of items to read in the weekly email newsletters, but only the most significant ones. |
| Community Context | Downside: Reading occurs in only one direction, and there are no other experts to provide context and review breaking vulnerabilities. | Benefit: Platforms like Mastodon, Reddit, or X support real-time discussion and peer review for breaking vulnerabilities. |

Task f) Port Scanning with Nmap

To understand how external entities map an organization's attack surface, we performed a port scanning exercise inside our isolated Debian 13 virtual machine.

### Step 1: Network Isolation
I disconnected the virtual machine from the internet and verified the offline status using `ping`.
```bash
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8

Baseline Port Scan (No Services Running)
Ran an aggressive nmap scan against localhost before installing any network daemons.
sudo nmap -A -v localhost

installed and started the Apache web server daemon locally:

Bash
sudo apt-get -y install apache2
sudo systemctl start apache2

With Apache active, re-ran the exact same nmap scan against localhost:

sudo nmap -A -v localhost

Analysis & Results:

Port 80/tcp was successfully identified as open (http).

The -A flag fingerprinted the underlying software version (Apache httpd 2.4.62 ((Debian))) and retrieved the web page title.

How criminals map services: Attackers and automated network scanners (like Shodan, Censys, or automated botnets) continuously probe public IP spaces. When a server responds on exposed ports (e.g., 80 for HTTP, 443 for HTTPS, 22 for SSH), the listening service returns a version banner, which attackers use to identify outdated software and match them with known vulnerabilities (CVEs)

